course: dictionary
lesson: 510
title: Assert and standing up for yourself
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To assert something is to say firmly that it is true. Firmly means strongly and clearly, with no doubt in your voice. «Assert your opinion with reasons» means say what you think and back it up with a good reason.
explain: Assert also means to speak up and act with confidence, so that others notice what you need or what is fair to you. In a story, «beginning to assert himself once more» shows the robin coming back to life and making himself heard again after a quiet time.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. The robin had been quiet, and then it began to act again. So assert means to show yourself clearly, not to hide. Try your meaning in the sentence: «beginning to show himself once more» still makes sense, so you have found it.
fact: Assert is a verb, and its forms are assert, asserts, asserted and asserting.
fact: «Assert» means to say firmly that something is true.
fact: To assert yourself means to speak up and act confidently so others notice your needs or rights.
fact: In maths and logic, to assert something means to state that it is true, and it may need proof.
fact: Being assertive means standing up for yourself politely, which is not the same as being aggressive, which means hurting or bullying others.
exercise: «The robin was beginning to assert himself once more.» What does assert himself mean here? | Fall silent for the winter | Hide quietly in the hedge | Forget how to sing | *Speak up and show he was back in charge of his patch
exercise: In maths, what does it mean to assert that a number pattern always works? | Draw a shape around the numbers | *State that it is true, which may need proof | Add all the numbers together quickly | Guess the answer and move on
exercise: In PSHE, what does being assertive mean? | Hiding your feelings so no one is upset | Doing whatever your friends say | *Standing up for yourself politely | Shouting until everyone agrees with you
test: In a debate, Ravi wants to assert his opinion. What is the best way to do it? | Agree with everyone straight away | Shout over the others until they stop | Say nothing at all | *Give a clear opinion and back it up with good reasons
test: Which word means to say firmly that something is true? | Drift | Wander | *Assert | Whisper
exam: Which sentence best describes someone who asserts their rights? | Someone who hides in the corner and never speaks | Someone who always lets others decide everything | *Someone who clearly and politely stands up for what is fair to them | Someone who gives away all their things
